# Copy to bridge.yaml and point it at your upstream.
#
# This file holds the upstream list and nothing else, because a list of objects
# is the one thing an environment variable cannot express. Every other setting
# is an environment variable, and the defaults are in brackets:
#
#   RAIL_PROXY_CONFIG_FILE   this file's path        (fastmcp_proxy/bridge.yaml,
#                            which nothing ships — mount or write one)
#   RAIL_PROXY_BIND          interface to listen on  (0.0.0.0)
#   RAIL_PROXY_PORT          port to listen on       (8091)
#   RAIL_PROXY_LOG_LEVEL                             (INFO)
#   RAIL_PROXY_UPSTREAM_TIMEOUT_SECONDS              (30)
#       How long a call waits on an upstream before the agent is told it timed
#       out.
#   RAIL_PLUGIN_ENABLED      true | false             (false)
#       Whether this proxy talks to a Rail Center at all. Off, it is a plain
#       proxy and attaches neither header. On, it attaches `x-rail` when a
#       ticket is held and `x-rail-status` when one could not be got.
#       Cross-checked against the three variables below, both ways: on with no
#       Rail Center to fetch from stops at startup, and so does off beside one
#       that is configured — which is what makes the off default safe, since a
#       deployment that loses the flag is one that still names a Rail Center.
#       Parsed strictly: nothing but `true` or `false`, case folded.
#
# Where a Rail Center issues this proxy a ticket, three name it — all three
# together, or none:
#
#   RAIL_CENTER_URL          e.g. https://rail-center.example.com
#   RAIL_HOST_ID             the host this proxy runs on
#   RAIL_SANDBOX_NAME        the agent sandbox it serves
#
# and these govern how it is fetched. `RAIL_AUTH_TOKEN` is required under
# `RAIL_AUTH_MODE=bearer` and ignored otherwise, and the override below does
# nothing unless a credential exists to send:
#
#   RAIL_AUTH_MODE           none | bearer            (none)
#   RAIL_AUTH_TOKEN          no default; `bearer` without one is refused
#   RAIL_PROXY_TICKET_TIMEOUT_SECONDS                (10)
#       Bounds each fetch, the one at startup included — and that one runs
#       before the port is bound, so it also bounds how long an unreachable
#       Rail Center delays that.
#   RAIL_PROXY_REFRESH_SECONDS                       (3600)
#       Upper bound between refreshes, not the interval — a ticket's own expiry
#       is what sets the cadence.
#   RAIL_PROXY_MAX_TICKET_LIFETIME_SECONDS           (no bound)
#       Refuse a ticket claiming a longer life than this.
#   RAIL_PROXY_ALLOW_INSECURE_CREDENTIAL             (false)
#       Send the credential to a plaintext, non-loopback Rail Center anyway.
#       Refused by default; SECURITY.md says what that refusal is for.
#
# `schema_version` is this file's format, and it is not the policy bundle's.
# Rail Center writes the bundle's; a release of this proxy writes what belongs
# here. They share the word so the concept is learned once, and they do not move
# together. The major is what is compared — a proxy reading 1.x takes any 1.y,
# ignoring whatever that minor added, and refuses a 2.x file outright rather
# than serving the parts it recognises. An upstream entry is the one place an
# unknown key is warned about. Omitting the version is read as 1.0 and warned
# about.
#
# `name` is the mount namespace, so the agent sees the upstream's tools as
# `<name>_<tool>`. Renaming it renames every tool. The namespace is
# unconditional — a single upstream is namespaced too, because two upstreams
# each exposing a `search` are indistinguishable in a `tools/call` without it,
# and a file that stopped namespacing once a second entry appeared would rename
# every tool of the first. FastMCP's mount takes an optional namespace and this
# proxy deliberately does not expose that knob: there is no value of this file
# that produces bare tool names.

schema_version: "1.0"

mcp:
  servers:
    - name: delivery
      url: http://gateway:8080/mcp
